      "author": "Tried By Fire",
      "authorHandle": "@TriedByFire",
      "text": "Right off the bat, we have the three main establishments we have already spoken about. The writer starts himself off by calling himself the “Preacher.” May we not forget that everything in scripture points to Jesus Christ, including this book of the Bible. I could argue that this book of the Bible is a mixture between the struggles with our own minds and wants, and the wants and mind of the Spirit in our daily walks. We will have to face struggles if we are going to follow Christ. The preacher could be the Holy Spirit referencing this to you. It’s a sobering reminder that we have a voice speaking to us from God’s Word, and it isn’t yourself that makes that voice. The Lord speaks through His Word. That is what a preacher does by the way; he speaks God’s Word. If a preacher hardly touches the word of God or doesn’t reference Christ in His sermon, I hardly think to call him a preacher at all. We will see that this preacher spoken about in this book of the Bible is very much influenced by the life and legacy of Jesus Christ and His finished work on the cross.",
