      "verse": 1,
      "startVerse": 1,
      "endVerse": 1,
      "author": "Tried By Fire",
      "authorHandle": "@TriedByFire",
      "text": "The last part of this verse states that his son was “kin in Jerusalem.” This phrase only applies to two sons. It could either be Absolom, who certainly wouldn’t have written this before his death, and there is no other information about the wisdom that pours out of this book of the Bible defining Absolom, or it could be Solomon. It’s a pretty obvious choice between the two. If you don’t know anything about Solomon, this book is probably not going to align historically accurately for you. It’s best to study up on Solomon in 1 Kings to get a pretty clear picture of who he was and why he needed to write this book of the Bible.",
